Part Five
Village Level Women and Children Protection Task Force Article 49 (1) The Village Level Women and Children Protection Task Force as referred to in Article 45 letter d constitutes a functional work unit that organizes integrated protection services and handling of cases of violence against children and women at the Village level.
(2) The Management of the Village Level Women and Children Protection Task Force as referred to in paragraph (1) consists of:
a. elements of Village apparatus;
b. elements of Village or Sub-district Supervising Non-Commissioned Officers;
c. elements of Community Supervising Non-Commissioned Officers;
d. community leaders or religious leaders;
e. youth elements; and
f. elements of the Village Family Welfare Development Mobilization Team. (3) The main duties of the Village Level Women and Children Protection Task Force as referred to in paragraph (1) are:
a. providing services in the context of protection of women and children;
b. receiving public complaints regarding cases of violence against women and children in the Village area;
c. facilitating women and children who are victims of violence in resolving their problems;
d. socializing to the community about the protection of women and children; and
e. providing assistance for victims or referring to the Sub-district level women and children protection task force.
(4) Further provisions regarding the establishment, duties and functions of the village level women and children protection task force as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be stipulated by village head decree.

3.4.evidence:
Article 18
(1) Children in emergency situations as referred to in Article 17 letter a, consist of:
a. children who become refugees;
b. children victims of riots;
c. children victims of natural disasters; and
d. children in armed conflict situations.
(2) The implementation of child protection in emergency situations as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be conducted in the form of:
a. Prevention Services, covering:
1) Making policies for child protection from all forms of violence, exploitation, neglect and maltreatment in emergency situations;
2) Making policies for fulfillment of basic needs consisting of food, clothing, shelter, education, health, learning and recreation, security guarantees, and equal treatment for children who become refugees, victims of violence, victims of natural disasters and children in armed conflict situations; and
3) Making policies and disaster management mechanisms that take into account the interests of children.
b. Risk Reduction Services, covering:
1) Conducting early identification, complaint services and assessment of children and families who are victims of violence, exploitation, neglect and maltreatment.
2) Providing services for children and families who are victims of violence, exploitation, neglect and maltreatment, covering:
a) legal consultation;
b) mediation to related parties;
c) psychosocial services;
d) economic assistance and life skills;
e) safe houses; and
f) shelters.
c. Case Handling Services, covering:
1) Conducting immediate rescue efforts for victims who are in dangerous conditions for their safety;
2) Providing referrals according to the needs of victims;
3) Providing counseling and support from families;
4) Treating children in emergency situations humanely in accordance with the dignity and rights of children;
5) Providing a sense of security and protection for children who become refugees, victims of violence, victims of natural disasters and children in armed conflict situations; and
6) Fulfilling special needs for children in emergency situations who experience psychosocial disorders.

